WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.810
Our class and the class next door both had

2
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:05.630
a mean of twelve in the science test.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.070
Same number, so same class?

4
00:00:08.890 --> 00:00:09.130
No.

5
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.460
In our class everyone was close to twelve.

6
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.620
In theirs, marks were all over the place.

7
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:20.030
Today: measuring how spread out data is,

8
00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.790
and spotting values that do not fit.

9
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.440
The standard deviation is the typical

10
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.380
distance of the values from the mean.

11
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.010
Small means the values

12
00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:32.550
huddle close to the mean.

13
00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:36.300
Large means they are spread far away.

14
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.400
To work it out, find each value's distance

15
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.700
from the mean, square it, take the mean of

16
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.020
the squares, and then the square root.

17
00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:49.050
The computer does this in one line.

18
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:51.210
Your job is to read it.

19
00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.130
Class A has a standard

20
00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.110
deviation of about one mark.

21
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.600
Class B has about five.

22
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.610
The same mean, but class B needs very

23
00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.630
different help for different pupils.

24
00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.030
An outlier is a value far from the rest.

25
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.570
A height of three hundred centimetres, or

26
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.510
a mark of ninety in a test out of twenty.

27
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.220
Outliers deserve a closer look

28
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.480
before anything else.

29
00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.690
One simple rule uses the

30
00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:23.970
middle half of the data.

31
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.420
Find the quartiles, and

32
00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:27.380
the gap between them.

33
00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:29.660
Anything more than one and a

34
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.480
half gaps beyond the quartiles

35
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.040
is flagged as an outlier.

36
00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:34.770
Quick check!

37
00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.850
The register says one pupil took three

38
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:39.630
hundred minutes to get to school.

39
00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:41.310
What should you do first?

40
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:43.640
Check it first.

41
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.760
It could be a typing mistake for thirty.

42
00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:50.010
Or it could be true, a pupil who

43
00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.390
walks from a far village.

44
00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.570
Never delete an outlier just

45
00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:55.790
because it is inconvenient.

46
00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.160
In the app, open The

47
00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.360
middle and the spread.

48
00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:03.610
Drag one mark far away, and

49
00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.430
watch how the range and the

50
00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.930
middle half react differently.

51
00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:08.760
Your turn.

52
00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.710
Class C has a standard deviation

53
00:02:11.710 --> 00:02:14.630
of two, and class D of six.

54
00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.160
Which class has more mixed marks?

55
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:19.210
Class D.

56
00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.650
Remember: the standard deviation is the

57
00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.290
typical distance from the mean.

58
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.000
Outliers are far from the rest.

59
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.550
Check them, explain them,

60
00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:30.950
and never hide them.

61
00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:34.670
Try it yourself: write down how long

62
00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.090
your journeys take for a week.

63
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.540
Find the values that stand out, and ask:

64
00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.640
is each one a mistake, or something real?
